Subject: [PATCH 11203/11362] [outer-loop] The memory index is over its read
 limit; I caused most of it

The hook shipped two hours ago fired on a subagent and surfaced what nobody was
looking for: MEMORY.md is larger than the limit at which it is read, so its
tail is silently dropped whenever a session loads it. Measured 27,429 bytes
against a 24,985-byte limit, nine entries invisible, including the
parse-don't-pattern-match lesson written tonight and cited repeatedly since.

I caused most of the overflow. The ten longest lines were nearly all mine from
tonight, up to 681 bytes against a 149-byte median. Fixing 'the index line does
not describe the body' I wrote summaries of whole files where the contract asks
for pointers, so I repaired a summary that lied by creating summaries that were
invisible, and the entry pushed off the end was the lesson I was applying.

Compressed my own lines in two passes with no entry removed, 152 before and
after: 27,429 to 25,493 bytes, nine invisible down to two. No detail lost;
every fact already lives in its file body.

Trimming cannot finish it. 152 entries at a 166-byte mean is 25,232 bytes
against a 24,985-byte limit, so 150 fit and the index is two entries over
capacity structurally. Filed as operator decision 16 with three options:
shorten entries that are not mine, merge or drop stale ones against the
never-prune doctrine, or split the index into a table of contents. Only the
third keeps scaling.

Also records that the hook-render verification succeeded: a subagent started
after the settings change quoted the reminder verbatim and confirmed it does
not fire on a frontmatter-only edit. Decision 14 now has its missing input.

